# python_models sweeps + figures (regenerate: sweeps then make_report_figures.py)
results/
python_models/results/

# nebula experiment output (regenerate: python -m nebula.experiments.<name>)
#
# G49, AS AMENDED 2026-08-06: "regenerable" is not the test. An experiment's
# output is TRACKED if any deliverable quotes a number from it — a results file
# that took longer to produce than it takes to review is an INPUT to the
# write-up, not a build artifact. `s9_yield_results.json` was in this list, and
# when session 11 wanted the per-design coordinates behind S9_YIELD.md §4 it
# cost 7560 fresh SPICE runs / 23 min to get back. It has been REMOVED from
# this list and is now tracked.
#
# Session 13 adds three more files that are TRACKED under the same rule, and
# they are listed here rather than left to silence so the choice is visible:
#   nebula/experiments/s9_yield_tail_results.json  (the re-run with a real tail)
#   nebula/experiments/tail_device_data.csv        (261 rows, TAIL_DEVICE.md 3-6)
#   nebula/experiments/tail_device_results.json
# Note also that `s9_yield.py`'s DEFAULT output name now depends on whether the
# tail is ideal, so session 12b's file cannot be overwritten by a later run with
# a different circuit in it. It already was once, during session 13's plumbing
# checks, and was only recoverable because it was tracked.
#
# The two still ignored here are ignored DELIBERATELY and at a known cost: the
# copies on disk are stale relative to the numbers BOUNDS_REDERIVATION.md and
# CL_SENSITIVITY.md publish, so tracking them would ship a file that disagrees
# with its own write-up. Their published figures are re-derivable only by
# re-running (~3 min per row, seeded).
s3_yield_results.json
cl_sensitivity_results.json
analytic_screen_results.json

# ngspice scratch. run_point() works in a TemporaryDirectory, but interactive
# sessions in nebula/device/spice/ leave these behind.
*.raw
*.log
swing.txt
nebula/device/spice/*.csv
nebula/device/spice/*.txt
nebula/device/spice/*.out

